  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 2d1e83780892b5c4592add1c4998da52, type: 3}
  m_Name: LiteratureFacts
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::FactPack
  category: 7
  facts:
  - Sherlock Holmes never said 'Elementary, my dear Watson'.
  - Mary Shelley was 18 when she wrote Frankenstein.
  - J.R.R. Tolkien invented new languages for his books.
  - The Great Gatsby sold poorly during F. Scott Fitzgerald's lifetime.
  - Alice's Adventures in Wonderland was banned in China.
  - Leo Tolstoy's War and Peace has over 500 characters.
  - Moby-Dick was based on a real white whale.
  - Charles Dickens always slept facing north.
  - "Victor Hugo's Les Mis\xE9rables has one of the longest sentences in literature."
  - Dr. Seuss invented the word 'nerd'.
  - Agatha Christie is the best-selling novelist of all time.
  - The first novel ever written is considered to be 'The Tale of Genji'.
  - George Orwell's '1984' was published in 1949.
